# ⛔ THIS BOX HAS `core.autocrlf=true` AND THIS REPO HAD NO ATTRIBUTES FILE, so
# git decided text-vs-binary by SNIFFING every blob. A font that ever sniffed as
# text would have every 0x0A rewritten to 0x0D 0x0A on checkout — a corrupt
# .woff2 that @font-face rejects SILENTLY, leaving the chart axis in a fallback
# face. That is the exact failure self-hosting the font was adopted to remove, so
# it is not left to a heuristic.
#
# (Sniffing does get it right today — `git diff --cached --numstat` reports `- -`
# for all four and the stored blobs are byte-identical to disk. This is here so
# that stays true for a font whose first 8 kB happens to contain no NUL.)
#
# The self-consistency rail is in tests/test_chart_parity_harness.py: a woff2
# header declares its own total length, and CRLF conversion breaks that equality.
*.woff2 binary
*.woff  binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary

# ⛔⛔ PINE SOURCES ARE BYTE-FIDELITY ARTIFACTS AND THIS BOX REWRITES LINE
# ENDINGS ON CHECKOUT. Measured 2026-09-11:
# `tests/fixtures/member/uncharted-volume.pine` was 34,950 bytes on disk against a
# 34,342-byte blob — exactly its 608 CRLFs — so
# `test_the_file_on_disk_matches_BOTH_recorded_hashes` failed on BOTH hashes and
# read as "🔴 THE AUTHOR'S SCRIPT CHANGED … every 'line N' reference in this
# project now points at a different statement". The author's script had not
# changed at all; the checkout filter had. Both hashes match the LF form exactly.
#
# ⚠️ THAT IS THE WORST DIRECTION FOR THIS PARTICULAR RAIL TO FAIL IN. It exists to
# shout when a member's script is swapped under our line-number references, so a
# line-ending false positive teaches whoever sees it that the loudest alarm in the
# fixture directory cries wolf — and the remedy it invites (re-record the hashes)
# would bake this box's CRLF into a manifest every other checkout then fails.
#
# Same class as the note-connector fixtures below, same fix. Applies to all 510
# tracked `.pine` files, not just the member two: the corpus rosters, the probe
# integrity sweep and the linemaps all compare Pine bytes or characters.
*.pine text eol=lf

# note-connectors cross-language schema rail (Task 5): the Python drift test
# (`test_note_convert_fixtures.py`) byte-compares a freshly regenerated copy
# of these fixtures against the committed ones. `fixtures_gen.py` always
# writes `\n`-only. Without `eol=lf` here, `core.autocrlf=true` (this box) on
# a future clone/checkout would silently rewrite the committed copies to
# CRLF, and the drift test would then report every fixture as "stale" for a
# line-ending reason that has nothing to do with converter drift — the same
# CRLF-only-on-Windows failure class as the chart-parity harness above.
api/services/journal_two/note_connectors/convert/fixtures_in/*.md text eol=lf
api/services/journal_two/note_connectors/convert/fixtures_in/*.html text eol=lf
app/src/pages/journal-2-0/lib/importer/__fixtures__/server_convert/*.json text eol=lf
# ⚰️ AND ITS SIBLING DIRECTORY, MISSED BY THE RULE ABOVE (added 2026-09-11).
# `test_obsidian_parity_fixtures.py` byte-compares a freshly regenerated copy against
# the committed one, exactly as the note-connector test does — but the eol rule named
# only `server_convert/`, so all SEVEN obsidian_parity fixtures were CRLF on disk
# against LF blobs and the test reported every one of them STALE.
# ⛔ ITS MESSAGE TELLS YOU TO REGENERATE AND COMMIT, which would have baked this
# box's CRLF into the repo and made the same test fail on every other checkout —
# a false alarm whose remedy causes the real thing. Measured: each file is
# byte-identical to its blob once line endings are normalised (e.g. 556 bytes on
# disk vs a 535-byte blob, exactly its 21 CRLFs). Nothing about the converter drifted.
app/src/pages/journal-2-0/lib/importer/__fixtures__/obsidian_parity/*.json text eol=lf

# ⛔⛔ THE CLASS, NOT THE INSTANCE — a DIRECTORY BACKSTOP for every fixture
# (added 2026-09-11, third instance of the same defect).
#
# The three rules above were each added the day one extension bit: `.pine` when
# the member-script hash rail cried wolf, `server_convert/*.json` for the
# note-connector drift test, `obsidian_parity/*.json` for its sibling. The fourth
# instance was `tests/fixtures/thinkscript/*.ts` — `ImportBox.thinkscript.test.jsx`
# byte-compares that file against what the import box produced, and CRLF on disk
# against an LF blob fails it on the very first statement: the box produced a
# bare-LF `declare upper;` line and the committed fixture arrived CRLF.
# (This comment deliberately spells no escape sequence: written with one, the
# escape became a REAL newline here and git parsed the remainder as a rule —
#   .gitattributes:71: 'declare is not a valid attribute name
# which is R8's defect class arriving in a config file instead of a message.)
# Fixing a fifth extension next week is not a fix; this is.
#
# ⭐ MEASURED before writing the rule, not guessed. 1,862 tracked files under
# tests/fixtures: json 1516 · pine 191 · py 89 · ts 24 · csv 16 · png 9 · md 9 ·
# jpg 5 · gitignore 2 · gz 1. And `git ls-files --eol` says EVERY text blob is
# ALREADY `i/lf` (1,615 of them checked out `w/crlf`, 230 `w/lf`). So this rule
# rewrites nothing in the index and changes no recorded hash — it stops the
# CHECKOUT from manufacturing a difference that was never committed.
tests/fixtures/** text eol=lf

# ⛔ …AND THE 15 BINARIES INSIDE IT, DECLARED AFTER SO THEY WIN. A later line
# overrides an earlier one, and `binary` expands to `-text`, so these are exempt
# from the rule above. Sweeping them into a text rule would rewrite every 0x0A
# inside a PNG on checkout — and these particular PNGs are the vendor VISUAL
# PARITY references, so a corrupted one would read as a product regression in the
# one comparison that exists to catch product regressions. That is the same
# failure the woff2 block at the top of this file was written to prevent; it
# named four font extensions and left the image formats to the sniffer.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.webp binary
*.ico  binary
*.gz   binary
